<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>New palpable left breast mass, clinically suspicious for cancer, in a postmenopausal woman of 52. Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound are booked this week, with core biopsy if indicated. Please see her once the results are available to discuss surgical options and axillary staging with her. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent suspected-cancer pathway, within two weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>If the biopsy confirms cancer, is she a candidate for breast-conserving surgery, and how would you present that choice and mastectomy to her?</item><item>Is breast MRI or any staging imaging needed before deciding?</item><item>How should her axilla be staged, and what would you do if the sentinel nodes are involved?</item><item>Should germline genetic testing be done before surgery?</item><item>What should she expect after surgery (radiation, endocrine therapy, and other systemic treatment), so that she can plan her work?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Carson is seen at the request of Dr. Vance-Okonkwo with a left breast mass she found about three weeks ago. Diagnostic mammography and ultrasound a week ago showed a 2.4 cm irregular spiculated mass at 2 o&apos;clock, BI-RADS 5, in breasts of scattered fibroglandular density, with normal axillary nodes on ultrasound. The core biopsy five days ago shows invasive ductal carcinoma, grade 2, ER 95 percent, PR 80 percent, HER2 1+ (negative), Ki-67 22 percent. She has no bone pain, cough, headaches, or weight loss. She had a benign left breast tumor removed about ten years ago. She asked at the start what the biopsy showed and was told directly that it is breast cancer; she asked to hear the options with their trade-offs before deciding. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Rachel Louise Carson was born on May 27, 1907, on a family farm in Springdale, Pennsylvania. [D]</item><item>Carson was hired by the U.S. Bureau of Fisheries in 1936 as a junior aquatic biologist and rose to be editor-in-chief of the publications of its successor, the U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. She resigned in 1952 to write full time. [D]</item><item>In 1950, while preparing her second book, Carson had her first breast operation, to remove a benign tumor from her left breast. [D]</item><item>In early 1960, while writing Silent Spring, Carson found two lumps in her left breast. [D]</item></list><paragraph>10 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

