    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Invasive ductal carcinoma of the left breast, clinical T2 N0 M0, hormone receptor positive, HER2 negative (C50.412). Early breast cancer suitable for surgery first. The tumor is single and can be removed with clear margins and a good appearance, so breast-conserving surgery followed by radiation gives the same survival as mastectomy. Both were explained to her with their trade-offs: radiation for several weeks with breast conservation, or no radiation in most cases but loss of the breast, with or without reconstruction, with mastectomy. She chose breast-conserving surgery. MRI is not needed for a single lesion in breasts that are not dense, and staging scans are not indicated at this stage without symptoms. [S]</item><item>Axillary staging. The axilla is normal on examination and ultrasound, so sentinel lymph node biopsy is the standard. If one or two sentinel nodes are involved, axillary dissection adds arm swelling without improving survival when whole-breast radiation follows; she would then have radiation that may include the regional nodes. [S]</item><item>Genetic risk. Germline testing is recommended for everyone with breast cancer diagnosed at 65 or younger. A pathogenic variant could change the operation, so the result should be known before surgery. [S]</item><item>What to expect after surgery. Endocrine therapy will be recommended because the cancer is strongly hormone receptor positive. Radiation follows breast conservation. Whether chemotherapy or a CDK4/6 inhibitor adds benefit depends on the nodal result and a recurrence score on the specimen; she will hear those numbers before any decision. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Diagnostic mammogram and ultrasound images and reports; core biopsy pathology with ER, PR, HER2, and Ki-67; Dr. Vance-Okonkwo&apos;s referral and examination. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Left lumpectomy with localization of the biopsy clip and sentinel lymph node biopsy within three weeks; no breast MRI and no staging scans. [S]</item><item>If one or two sentinel nodes are involved, no axillary dissection, with whole-breast radiation and regional nodal radiation considered by radiation oncology. [S]</item><item>Germline multigene panel testing with genetic counseling now, with the result before surgery. [S]</item><item>Baseline arm measurements before surgery for later lymphedema checks; acetaminophen rather than nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs after surgery because of her recent ulcer. [S]</item><item>A recurrence score on the surgical specimen and review at the multidisciplinary breast clinic with medical and radiation oncology, who will give her the expected benefit and side effects of each option in numbers. [S]</item><item>Every result to be given to her directly, in plain words and with a written copy, before it is shared with anyone else, and only with the people she names. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Vance-Okonkwo the same day; Ms. Carson has a written summary of her choice and the next steps and agreed to the plan. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Breast surgical oncology will see her at the pre-operative visit with the genetic result, and one week after surgery with the pathology. Primary care continues distress screening and coordinates the oncology reviews. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
